    "case_id": "52",
    "case_summary": "A 48-year-old female presents with an enlargement of her anterior neck which made swallowing very difficult for 2 weeks now. She has had constipation for the past 6 weeks and overall fatigue. She also had heavy menstrual bleeding; and often feels extremely cold at home. On the other hand, she has well-controlled asthma and spring allergies. On examination, the thyroid is stony hard, tender and asymmetrically enlarged. There is also pain associated with swallowing. Laboratory studies show a serum T4 level of 4.4 ≈í¬∫g/dL and a TSH level of 6.3 mU/L. A radionuclide thyroid scanning indicates that the nodule has low radioactive iodine uptake. What is the most likely pathology to be found in this patient?\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Graves' disease\nB. Hashimoto's thyroiditis\nC. thyroid nodules\nD. thyroid cancer\nE. subacute thyroiditis\nF. Reidel thyroiditis\nG. None\n",
